     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. 6400 mg a day of oral ibuprofen is twice the adult maximum, and I don't know of any accepted indication for 1600 mg four times daily. The closest thing is high-dose ibuprofen for cystic fibrosis, but that's dosed by weight and given twice daily, not four times. A dose this high carries a real risk of GI bleeding, acute kidney injury and other serious harm, so I'm not comfortable entering it on confirmation alone.\n\nHere's what I can do:\n1. **Enter it at or below the maximum.** For example, 800 mg four times daily (3200 mg a day) or 600 mg four times daily (2400 mg a day). I can do this right away.\n2. **Enter a different drug or add another painkiller** if pain control is the goal. Options include scheduled acetaminophen or a different approach to pain.\n3. **Have pharmacy or the attending review it.** If there's a specific clinical reason for this dose, such as a protocol or a specialist recommendation, a pharmacist can check it and enter it through the usual override process.\n\nWhich would you like?"
      }
